*\n\nNow I'm completely sure, each *connected* component of \"2\" has its own bounding box, which is then expanded. Only the perimeter cells that are initially 0 are changed to 1. This means disconnected \"2\"s are bordered separately, even if their expanded boxes overlap. The rules are now confirmed across all given examples! Now I can build the exact bordering logic I need.\n\n\n**Validating Border Algorithm**\n\nI've just run through Example 2's top-left to check my bordering algorithm, confirming it works as expected! I've now formalized the rules. I'm confident in the logic, which involves finding connected components of '2', expanding their bounding boxes by one, and setting any zero-valued perimeter cells to one. The algorithm correctly handles the bounding box expansion and zero-value overwrite.\n\n\n",
